      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Key figures in 2020",
      "pageStart": 8,
      "pageEnd": 8,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-key-figures-in-2020.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "was established in 2015 as a key component of the \nreforms undertaken by WHO to strengthen its role in \nemergencies. The unique benefit of the CFE is that it \ncan be used to rapidly deploy WHO and global health \nemergency workforce assets for control and containment \nof a disease outbreak or to strengthen the health \nresponse in humanitarian crises before other funding \nmechanisms can be triggered. The CFE is a global public \ngood in that it addresses health emergencies that can \nhave regional and global impact. The ability to respond \nrapidly to an outbreak or an emergency can make the \ndiffe",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that the Contingency Fund for Emergencies was set up in 2015 to let WHO deploy resources quickly before other funding mechanisms such as CERF are available, releasing funds within 24 hours. It notes that releases cover response activities, staff and supplies and are subject to WHO financial rules and annual reporting.",
        "state": "draft",
