      "target": "../sections/sec-011-life-saving-health-response-to-tropical-cyclone.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "©WHO / Philippe Metois\nCFE funds supported Vanuatu Ministry of Health activities in response to Tropical Cyclone Harold.\nWHO Vanuatu provided critical rapid response support, \ndespite COVID-19 restrictions which led to delays in the \narrival of key resources and a lack of military logistical \nsupport. This included, for example, WHO Vanuatu \nsupport for the deployment of EMTs, through the Vanuatu \nMedical Assistance Team in the first four weeks of the \nresponse. It also included direct financial contributions \nto front-line responders in the Ministry of Health to cover \ntravel costs and daily",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes WHO support in Vanuatu after Cyclone Harold despite COVID-19 restrictions, including emergency medical team deployment, surveillance tools and repairs to three health facilities, later reimbursed by CERF. It then outlines the health crisis in north-eastern Syria after displacement in 2019, where few health centres were fully functional and WHO used the fund to airlift supplies when borders closed.",
